# Coding Conventions

## Language

- All code is in English: variable names, function names, file names, comments, and text the program prints.
- This app has no locale files. A printed sentence lives next to the code that prints it.

```python
# BAD — a non-English name, and the sentence lives in another table
nombre = config["nombre"]
print(messages["esperando"])

# GOOD — English names. The sentence sits next to the print.
player_name = config["player_name"]
print(f"Waiting for {player_name}")
```

## Formatting

- Format Python with Black at its default line length, 88.
- Do not hand-wrap lines to a different width.

```python
# BAD — broken by hand at a shorter width
raise SystemExit(
    "threshold_percent, countdown_seconds, "
    "and overlay_port must be numbers."
)

# GOOD — one line. Black wraps it when it passes 88.
raise SystemExit("threshold_percent, countdown_seconds, and overlay_port must be numbers.")
```

## DRY

- Before adding logic, look in the file and the rest of the package for a function that already does the job, and reuse it.
- When the same operation appears twice, extract it into a named function placed above its callers.
- A function that would have only one caller in the same file is inlined instead. See [inline-single-use.mdc](inline-single-use.mdc).

```python
# BAD — the same read copied in two places
def load_settings(path):
    return json.loads(Path(path).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))

def load_backup(path):
    return json.loads(Path(path).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))

# GOOD — one function, placed above both callers
def load_json(path):
    return json.loads(Path(path).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))

settings = load_json(settings_path)
backup = load_json(backup_path)
```
